  [1mDedupe key: [38;5;153mtaskId:ruleId:firedFor[39m,[0m where [38;5;153mfiredFor[39m is the due instant the event concerns — not when it was minted.
  This is the design's best trick. Re-running [38;5;153mevaluate()[39m a thousand times mints nothing new, because the key is stable.
  But move a task's due date from Tuesday to Friday and [38;5;153mfiredFor[39m changes, so a fresh event mints — which is correct —
  while Tuesday's event remains as a true record of what was believed then. [1mInvalidation is solved by key construction [0m
  [1mrather than by cancelling scheduled rows[0m, which is precisely the bug class that made approach C unattractive.

  [1mTwo rules for v1:[0m [38;5;153mdue-soon[39m (fires at [38;5;153mdueAt − leadTime[39m, default 60 min) and [38;5;153moverdue[39m (fires at [38;5;153mdueAt[39m). Both require an
  incomplete task with reminders on. Completing a task suppresses future firings but never deletes existing events.

  [1mCatch-up, and the distinction that matters.[0m [38;5;153mevaluate()[39m runs on load, on a 30-second tick, and on tab refocus. Because
  it's a pure function of (tasks, seen keys, now), a tab opened after three days mints exactly the events that came true
  meanwhile — once each. But [1mminting is not alerting.[0m Forty catch-up events must not become forty toasts. So events
  always land in the inbox and always count toward unread, while the toast surface fires only for events minted this
  session whose [38;5;153mfiredFor[39m is inside a 24-hour recency window. Older ones arrive silently under one summary line: [3m"12 [0m
  [3mreminders while you were away."[0m

  [1mRetention,[0m because an append-only log in a 5 MB store is not free: on startup, prune dismissed events older than 90
  days and cap the log at 1000, dropping oldest-dismissed then oldest-read. [1mNever prune unread[0m — if the cap would
  require it, warn instead of silently discarding.

  One genuine judgment call I'd rather you make than assume:
